"""Interview questions with what they check and a sample answer, by subject, for /interview-questions/<role>/.

Why (search and AI answers check, 18 Nov 2026): for "data analyst interview questions" Bing's answer lists about fifty questions in
seven groups (SQL, statistics, cleaning, visualization, business thinking) and the first results carry 54 to 80 with answers. The page
had questions made from the role's skills and duties and the SQL and Python tests; it had nothing on statistics, charts or business
cases, and the other fields of work had no questions of their own trade.

Each subject: (heading, who gets it, questions). Who gets it: `fams` (role families of roles_info.FAMILIES), `roles` (role slugs, which
get it whatever their family), `tags` (skills: the subject is added when a tenth of the role's postings name one of them), `not_roles`.
A question is (question, what it checks, what a good answer shows, a sample answer). The sample answers are examples of the form, as the
resume examples are: no employer is named and the figures are invented. Facts stated in them (what a p-value is, what a join does) are
textbook facts and must stay exact: run `python3 interview_bank.py` after any edit.

OWN (interview_more.json): questions of one kind of work only, shown first on its page. Why (20 Nov 2026): the subjects above are
shared by a whole field, so the actuary page had accounting questions and none about reserves or pricing. They were drafted by Claude
in one batch (ai_fill.py interview) under the rules of test(), then each set was read by a second request that looked only for wrong
or doubtful facts; every question it doubted was left out.
"""
import json
from pathlib import Path

